  "title": "LEKTION 19: UNREGELMÄSSIGE VERBEN",
  "header_color": "cyan",
  "pages": [
   {
    "page": 1,
    "total_pages": 2,
    "content": [
     "Die meisten Verben bilden das Präteritum",
     "mit -ed. Aber die häufigsten Verben",
     "brechen diese Regel und ändern ihre",
     "Form:",
     "",
     "   go    ->  went                    Drücke 1",
     "",
     "   say   ->  said                    Drücke 2",
     "",
     "   take  ->  took                    Drücke 3",
     "",
     "   come  ->  came                    Drücke 4",
     "",
     "Es gibt keinen Trick zum Raten. Du",
     "lernst jedes einzeln, sobald es dir",
     "begegnet - wie ein neues Wort."
    ],
    "pronunciation_mappings": {
     "1": "Go. Went.",
     "2": "Say. Said.",
     "3": "Take. Took.",
     "4": "Come. Came."
    }
   },
   {
    "page": 2,
    "total_pages": 2,
    "content": [
     "Im Satz sehen sie so aus:",
     "",
     "   She went home late.               Drücke 1",
     "   He said no to me.                 Drücke 2",
     "   I took her hand.                  Drücke 3",
     "   They came back for us.            Drücke 4",
     "",
     "Weitere kommen Karte für Karte:",
     "break->broke, speak->spoke,",
     "buy->bought, lose->lost.",
     "",
     "Deutsch kennt das auch: die starken",
     "Verben ändern ihren Stammvokal",
     "(gehen -> ging, nehmen -> nahm). Genau",
     "die häufigen Verben sind die wilden.",
     "",
     "Wenige, alt und überall - was du einmal",
     "lernst, behältst du für immer."
    ],
    "pronunciation_mappings": {
     "1": "She went home late.",
     "2": "He said no to me.",
     "3": "I took her hand.",
     "4": "They came back for us."
    }
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "lesson_id": "020",
  "trigger_word": "city",
  "trigger_card_number": 351,
  "title": "LEKTION 20: FRAGEN MIT DID",
  "header_color": "cyan",
  "pages": [
   {
    "page": 1,
    "total_pages": 2,
    "content": [
     "Um in der Vergangenheit zu fragen oder",
     "zu verneinen, nimmst du did - die",
     "Vergangenheitsform des Hilfsworts do.",
     "",
     "FRAGE: did rückt nach vorn.",
     "",
     "   Did you see it?                   Drücke 1",
     "   Did she call you?                 Drücke 2",
     "",
     "NEIN: did + not (didn't) vor dem Verb.",
     "",
     "   I didn't know.                    Drücke 3",
     "   We didn't wait.                   Drücke 4",
     "",
     "Achtung: das Hauptverb kehrt zur",
     "Grundform zurück. did trägt schon die",
     "Vergangenheit - markiere sie nicht",
     "zweimal."
    ],
    "pronunciation_mappings": {
     "1": "Did you see it?",
     "2": "Did she call you?",
     "3": "I didn't know.",
     "4": "We didn't wait."
    }
   },
   {
    "page": 2,
    "total_pages": 2,
    "content": [
     "Nur EIN Vergangenheits-Zeichen pro Satz",
     "- did ODER das Vergangenheitsverb, nie",
     "beide:",
     "",
     "   Did you go?      (nicht: did you went)",
     "   I didn't see it. (nicht: didn't saw)",
     "",
     "Be braucht kein did. Es stellt sich",
     "selbst um oder nimmt not:",
     "",
     "   Were you there?   It wasn't ready.",
     "",
     "Deutsch kennt kein did: du drehst einfach",
     "das Verb um (Sahst du es?). Englisch",
     "schiebt did davor - das gibt es im",
     "Deutschen nicht."
    ]
   }
  ]
 }
]
